1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,846
What if your Twitch chat
could invent the next game?

2
00:00:03,846 --> 00:00:10,000
Vibe Arena brings their ideas, their votes
and the people behind the game onto the stream.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,891
Here is the live path.
Viewers join from Twitch,

4
00:00:14,891 --> 00:00:18,696
or stay in chat and send commands.

5
00:00:18,696 --> 00:00:24,674
In Community Grove, browser players and chat
grow the same garden.

6
00:00:24,674 --> 00:00:31,196
The credit strip keeps the game creator,
artist and idea author visible.

7
00:00:31,196 --> 00:00:35,000
This art is from Kenney's CC0 collection.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,182
In the Studio, viewers propose ideas and vote.

9
00:00:39,182 --> 00:00:43,364
The streamer reviews the selected idea
and resources,

10
00:00:43,364 --> 00:00:45,977
then approves that exact input.

11
00:00:45,977 --> 00:00:49,114
Changing the selection means reviewing again.

12
00:00:49,114 --> 00:00:54,864
Input-round credits show the idea authors,
supporting vote count and approver,

13
00:00:54,864 --> 00:00:58,000
separately from the game already playing.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,657
The chat participates;
the streamer keeps the final decision.

15
00:01:03,657 --> 00:01:07,429
Credits follow the version actually playing.

16
00:01:07,429 --> 00:01:13,714
I built the prototype with Devin CLI
before the hackathon.

17
00:01:13,714 --> 00:01:20,000
Codex wrote the hackathon code;
Devin reviewed and discussed ideas.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,559
This is our real Lantern Table trial.

19
00:01:23,559 --> 00:01:28,136
I selected its idea;
it had zero audience votes.

20
00:01:28,136 --> 00:01:31,695
Codex built it in one isolated VM,

21
00:01:31,695 --> 00:01:36,780
another VM passed nine protocol checks,
and both were destroyed.

22
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:40,847
I approved the exact version
as reviewed evidence.

23
00:01:40,847 --> 00:01:45,424
Dispatch through testing and cleanup
took about sixty-three seconds.

24
00:01:45,424 --> 00:01:50,000
This preview is static;
the runtime VM comes next.

25
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,545
If something fails, the watchdog
switches to a reviewed fallback.

26
00:01:54,545 --> 00:01:59,091
A real Twitch moderator can also request it
with fallback.

27
00:01:59,091 --> 00:02:03,182
Here is that requested recovery,
with the reason visible

28
00:02:03,182 --> 00:02:05,000
and players still connected.

29
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,333
This is my separate teeworlds-rs game,

30
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:12,778
built with Devin and Hermes,
outside this submission.

31
00:02:12,778 --> 00:02:16,667
The next experience is streamer versus chat:

32
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:18,889
players join as tees,

33
00:02:18,889 --> 00:02:25,000
while spectators send buffs and hazards
through an isolated game adapter.

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,390
Next are isolated runtime VMs
and a general adapter

35
00:02:29,390 --> 00:02:31,341
for verified external games,

36
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:34,268
including community submissions
with behavior reports.

37
00:02:34,268 --> 00:02:38,659
Then live repair with regression checks
and exact approval,

38
00:02:38,659 --> 00:02:41,098
stream statistics, and lasting credits

39
00:02:41,098 --> 00:02:45,000
for artists, musicians, writers
and every creative role.
